<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qabala</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gabala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qabala is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> on the southern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a>. Near it lie the ruins of ancient Kabala, the early capital of Caucasian Albania, mentioned by Pliny and Ptolemy, with walls and gates excavated by archaeologists. The capital later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barda">Partav</a>, modern Barda, but Kabala remained an important town into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qabala.jpg" alt="Qabala" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sefer azeri · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Q%C9%99b%C9%99l%C9%99_%C5%9F%C9%99h%C9%99rinin_Tufanda%C4%9Fdan_g%C3%B6r%C3%BCn%C3%BC%C5%9F%C3%BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
